select public.fd_ai_upsert_chunks('fd-textbook', '1b1a21163698', 13, $aim_json$[{"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.0#1", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.0", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.0", "heading": "Overview", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 1, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.0 Overview", "body": "Black, F. (1989). How we came up with the option formula. Journal of Portfolio Management, 15(2), 4–8.\nBlack, F., and Scholes, M. (1973). The pricing of options and corporate liabilities. Journal of Political Economy, 81(3), 637–654.\nHull, J. C. (2022). Options, Futures, and Other Derivatives (11th ed.). Pearson. Chapter 15.\nMacKenzie, D. (2006). An Engine, Not a Camera: How Financial Models Shape Markets. MIT Press.\nMerton, R. C. (1973). Theory of rational option pricing. Bell Journal of Economics and Management Science, 4(1), 141–183."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.0#2", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.0", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.0", "heading": "Decide first", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 2, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.0 Decide first", "body": "⚙ Decision — choose first; the theory follows\nHow much protection should the pension fund buy?\nThe Northwind pension fund holds a $12,000,000 equity portfolio that moves with a stock index, now at 6,000. The trustees wish to limit the loss over the next three months without selling the portfolio. Put options on the index are available at any strike; each contract pays $100 for every index point by which the index finishes below the strike, and twenty contracts cover the portfolio. Select the level of the index below which the fund is to be protected.\nProtection against a fall of more than 10 percent costs less than half of one percent of the portfolio; protection against any fall at all costs more than 3 percent for a single quarter. The choice of strike is a choice of how much risk to retain, exactly as the deductible is in an insurance policy. The prices in the figure come from a formula that differs from that of Chapter 12 in one respect only: the index pays dividends. This chapter develops that modification and applies it to three kinds of underlying asset."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.1#3", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.1", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.1", "heading": "Options on an asset that provides a known yield", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 3, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.1 Options on an asset that provides a known yield", "body": "Chapter 12 valued options on a share that pays no dividend. Stock indices, currencies, and futures contracts all differ from such a share in one respect: the holder of the underlying asset receives income, or its equivalent, during the life of the option. Section 4.4 introduced the appropriate description. An asset provides a yield at the rate \\(q\\) per year, with continuous compounding, if income is paid continuously at the rate \\(q\\) multiplied by the current price.\nThe key to all the results of this chapter is a single observation. Consider two assets with the same price at time \\(T\\): asset A, which provides a yield at the rate \\(q\\) and is now worth \\(S_0\\), and asset B, which provides no income. An investor who holds one unit of asset A and reinvests the income in the asset will hold \\(e^{qT}\\) units at time \\(T\\). To hold exactly one unit at \\(T\\) it is sufficient to buy \\(e^{-qT}\\) units today, at a cost of \\(S_0e^{-qT}\\). Asset B, which delivers the same thing at \\(T\\), must therefore be worth \\(S_0e^{-qT}\\) today.\nPrinciple 13.1"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.1#4", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.1", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.1", "heading": "Options on an asset that provides a known yield", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 4, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.1 Options on an asset that provides a known yield", "body": "A European option on an asset with price \\(S_0\\) that provides a yield at the rate \\(q\\) has the same value as the corresponding European option on an asset with price \\(S_0e^{-qT}\\) that provides no income. Every result for options on a share that pays no dividend therefore applies, with \\(S_0\\) replaced by \\(S_0e^{-qT}\\).\nApplying the principle to the results of Chapter 8 gives the lower bounds\n\\[ c\\ \\ge\\ \\max\\!\\left(S_0e^{-qT}-Ke^{-rT},\\,0\\right),\\qquad p\\ \\ge\\ \\max\\!\\left(Ke^{-rT}-S_0e^{-qT},\\,0\\right), \\]\nand put–call parity in the form\n\\[ c+Ke^{-rT}=p+S_0e^{-qT}. \\]\nApplying it to Result 12.2, and noting that \\(\\ln(S_0e^{-qT}/K)=\\ln(S_0/K)-qT\\), gives the pricing formulas.\nResult 13.1 — European options on an asset that provides a yield\n\\[ c=S_0e^{-qT}N(d_1)-Ke^{-rT}N(d_2),\\qquad p=Ke^{-rT}N(-d_2)-S_0e^{-qT}N(-d_1), \\] \\[ d_1=\\frac{\\ln(S_0/K)+(r-q+\\sigma^{2}/2)T}{\\sigma\\sqrt T},\\qquad d_2=d_1-\\sigma\\sqrt T . \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.1#5", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.1", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.1", "heading": "Options on an asset that provides a known yield", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 5, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.1 Options on an asset that provides a known yield", "body": "These formulas were first given by Merton in 1973. In terms of risk-neutral valuation, the total return on every asset in a risk-neutral world is \\(r\\); if the asset provides income at the rate \\(q\\), its price must grow at the rate \\(r-q\\), which is the quantity that appears in \\(d_1\\). This is the same adjustment that Table 10.1 made to the binomial tree. For an American option the formulas do not apply, and the tree of Chapter 10 with \\(a=e^{(r-q)\\Delta t}\\) is used."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.2#6", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.2", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.2", "heading": "Options on stock indices", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 6, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.2 Options on stock indices", "body": "Options on stock indices trade in very large volume on exchanges and over the counter. The most active are the options on the S&P 500 index listed by Cboe Global Markets, which are European and are settled in cash: on exercise, the holder of a call receives \\((S-K)\\times100\\) dollars and the holder of a put \\((K-S)\\times100\\) dollars, where \\(S\\) is the level of the index at settlement. No shares change hands. Options on the EURO STOXX 50, the FTSE 100, and the Nikkei 225 are designed in the same way, with their own multipliers.\nAn index is treated as an asset that provides a yield equal to the dividend yield on its constituent shares during the life of the option, and Result 13.1 applies with \\(q\\) set to that yield."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.2#7", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.2", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.2", "heading": "Options on stock indices", "kind": "example", "ord": 7, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.2 Options on stock indices", "body": "Worked example 13.1 — A put on a stock index\nA stock index stands at 6,000 and has a volatility of 18 percent and a dividend yield of 1.2 percent. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. Value a three-month European put with a strike of 5,700.\n1\nPreliminaries. \\(\\ln(6{,}000/5{,}700)=0.05129\\); \\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.18\\times0.5=0.09\\); \\(S_0e^{-qT}=6{,}000e^{-0.003}=5{,}982.03\\); \\(Ke^{-rT}=5{,}700e^{-0.01}=5{,}643.28\\).\n2\n\\(d_1\\) and \\(d_2\\). \\(d_1=\\dfrac{0.05129+(0.04-0.012+0.0162)\\times0.25}{0.09}=\\dfrac{0.05129+0.01105}{0.09}=0.6927\\); \\(d_2=0.6927-0.09=0.6027\\).\n3\nNormal probabilities. \\(N(-d_1)=0.2442\\); \\(N(-d_2)=0.2734\\).\n4\nPut. \\(p=5{,}643.28\\times0.2734-5{,}982.03\\times0.2442=1{,}542.61-1{,}461.10=81.51\\) index points. One contract costs \\(81.51\\times100=\\$8{,}151\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.2#8", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.2", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.2", "heading": "Portfolio insurance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 8, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.2 Portfolio insurance", "body": "The principal use of index puts by institutions is to limit the loss on an equity portfolio, as in the opening exercise. The strategy, a protective put on a portfolio, is called portfolio insurance.\nConsider first a portfolio whose beta is 1.0, so that its value moves in proportion to the index. If the portfolio is worth \\(V\\) and the index stands at \\(S_0\\), the portfolio is equivalent to \\(V/S_0\\) units of the index, and each put contract protects 100 units. The number of contracts required is\n\\[ \\frac{V}{100\\,S_0}. \\]\nFor the pension fund, \\(12{,}000{,}000/(100\\times6{,}000)=20\\) contracts. With a strike of 5,700 they cost \\(20\\times100\\times81.5114=\\$163{,}023\\), or 1.36 percent of the portfolio. If the index finishes below 5,700, the portfolio is worth \\(12{,}000{,}000\\times S_T/6{,}000=2{,}000\\,S_T\\) and the puts pay \\(20\\times100\\times(5{,}700-S_T)=2{,}000\\times(5{,}700-S_T)\\); the total is \\(2{,}000\\times5{,}700=\\$11{,}400{,}000\\) whatever the level of the index."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.2#9", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.2", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.2", "heading": "Portfolio insurance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 9, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.2 Portfolio insurance", "body": "When the beta of the portfolio is not 1.0, two adjustments are needed. The number of contracts becomes \\(\\beta V/(100S_0)\\), since the portfolio moves \\(\\beta\\) times as far as the index. And the strike must be chosen so that the puts begin to pay at the level of the index at which the portfolio reaches the value to be protected, which is found from the relation between the return on the portfolio and the return on the index used in Section 5.5."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.2#10", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.2", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.2", "heading": "Portfolio insurance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 10, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.2 Portfolio insurance", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 13.1 — Portfolio insurance at expiration\nThe insured portfolio has a floor; the uninsured portfolio does not\nThe fund holds 20 puts with a strike of 5,700, bought for $163,023. Set the level of the index in three months and examine the value of the portfolio, the payoff of the puts, and the net result."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.2#11", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.2", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.2", "heading": "Portfolio insurance", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 11, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.2 Portfolio insurance", "body": "Concept check 13.1\nA fund manager holds a portfolio worth $30,000,000 with a beta of 1.0. The index stands at 5,000 and each index option contract has a multiplier of $100. How many put contracts insure the portfolio, and which position is taken?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.3#12", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.3", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.3", "heading": "Options on currencies", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 12, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.3 Options on currencies", "body": "A currency option gives the right to buy or sell a specified amount of a foreign currency at a specified exchange rate. Most are traded over the counter, where corporations buy them from banks with strikes, amounts, and dates of their own choosing. As in Section 4.6, let \\(S_0\\) be the price of one unit of the foreign currency in the domestic currency, and \\(r_f\\) the foreign risk-free rate. A holding of foreign currency earns interest at \\(r_f\\); the currency is an asset that provides a yield, and Result 13.1 applies with \\(q=r_f\\). In this form the formulas are known as the Garman–Kohlhagen model.\nThe formulas may be written more compactly in terms of the forward exchange rate. From Result 4.5, \\(F_0=S_0e^{(r-r_f)T}\\), so that \\(S_0e^{-r_fT}=F_0e^{-rT}\\), and\n\\[ c=e^{-rT}\\left[F_0N(d_1)-KN(d_2)\\right],\\qquad p=e^{-rT}\\left[KN(-d_2)-F_0N(-d_1)\\right],\\qquad d_1=\\frac{\\ln(F_0/K)+\\sigma^{2}T/2}{\\sigma\\sqrt T}. \\]"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.3#13", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.3", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.3", "heading": "Options on currencies", "kind": "example", "ord": 13, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.3 Options on currencies", "body": "Worked example 13.2 — Hedging a payment in euros with a call\nNorthwind must pay €5,000,000 to a German supplier in six months. The exchange rate is $1.1000 per euro; the risk-free rates are 4.5 percent in dollars and 2.5 percent in euros; and the volatility of the exchange rate is 9 percent. Northwind wishes to limit the dollar cost without giving up the benefit of a fall in the euro. Value a six-month European call on the euro with a strike of $1.1200, and describe the result of the hedge.\n1\nPreliminaries. \\(\\ln(1.10/1.12)=-0.01802\\); \\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.09\\times0.7071=0.06364\\); \\(S_0e^{-r_fT}=1.10e^{-0.0125}=1.08634\\); \\(Ke^{-rT}=1.12e^{-0.0225}=1.09508\\).\n2\n\\(d_1\\) and \\(d_2\\). \\(d_1=\\dfrac{-0.01802+(0.045-0.025+0.00405)\\times0.5}{0.06364}=\\dfrac{-0.01802+0.01203}{0.06364}=-0.0942\\); \\(d_2=-0.1578\\).\n3\nCall. \\(N(d_1)=0.4625\\); \\(N(d_2)=0.4373\\). \\(c=1.08634\\times0.4625-1.09508\\times0.4373=0.50242-0.47888=\\$0.02353\\) per euro.\n4\nCost of the hedge. \\(5{,}000{,}000\\times0.02353=\\$117{,}650\\), or 2.1 percent of the amount due at the spot rate.\n5"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.3#14", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.3", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.3", "heading": "Options on currencies", "kind": "example", "ord": 14, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.3 Options on currencies", "body": "Result. If the euro is above $1.12 in six months, Northwind exercises and pays \\(5{,}000{,}000\\times1.12=\\$5{,}600{,}000\\). If it is below, the option expires and Northwind buys euros at the lower market rate. The dollar cost cannot exceed $5,600,000 plus the premium. A forward contract at \\(1.10e^{0.01}=\\$1.1111\\) would have fixed the cost at $5,555,300 with no premium, and with no benefit from a fall in the euro."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.3#15", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.3", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.3", "heading": "Options on currencies", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 15, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.3 Options on currencies", "body": "A call on one currency is a put on the other. The right to buy €5,000,000 for $5,600,000 is the right to sell $5,600,000 for €5,000,000: a call on the euro with a strike of $1.12 is a put on the dollar with a strike of \\(1/1.12=\\)€0.8929. The two descriptions refer to the same contract and must give the same value when it is expressed in a common currency.\nConcept check 13.2\nA Japanese exporter will receive $10,000,000 in three months and will convert it into yen. It wishes to be protected against a fall in the dollar while retaining the benefit of a rise. Which contract is appropriate?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.4#16", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.4", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.4", "heading": "Options on futures", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 16, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.4 Options on futures", "body": "A futures option, or option on futures, is the right to enter a futures contract at a specified futures price. Futures options are American and are traded on the exchange that lists the underlying futures contract. They are the principal form of exchange-traded option on commodities and on interest rates."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.4#17", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.4", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.4", "heading": "Mechanics", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 17, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.4 Mechanics", "body": "When a call futures option is exercised, the holder acquires a long position in the underlying futures contract together with a cash amount equal to the excess of the most recent settlement futures price over the strike price. When a put futures option is exercised, the holder acquires a short position in the futures contract together with cash equal to the excess of the strike price over the most recent settlement futures price. The futures position has zero value when it is acquired and may be closed at once, so that the effective payoff from a call is \\(\\max(F-K,0)\\) and from a put \\(\\max(K-F,0)\\), where \\(F\\) is the futures price at the time of exercise.\nSuppose that Northwind holds a call on one copper futures contract of 25,000 pounds with a strike of $4.20, and exercises when the settlement futures price is $4.45 and the current futures price is $4.47. Northwind receives a long futures position and cash of \\((4.45-4.20)\\times25{,}000=\\$6{,}250\\). If it closes the futures position immediately at $4.47, it gains a further \\((4.47-4.45)\\times25{,}000=\\$500\\), for a total of \\((4.47-4.20)\\times25{,}000=\\$6{,}750\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.4#18", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.4", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.4", "heading": "Reasons for their use", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 18, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.4 Reasons for their use", "body": "Options on futures are often preferred to options on the underlying asset itself. The futures contract is usually more liquid than the asset and its price is known at every moment from trading on the exchange, whereas the spot price of a commodity may be available only from dealers. Exercise does not ordinarily lead to delivery of the asset, since the futures position is closed beforehand; this is of particular value for commodities. And the option and the futures contract trade on the same exchange, which facilitates hedging and arbitrage between them."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.4#19", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.4", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.4", "heading": "Valuation: Black's model", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 19, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.4 Valuation: Black's model", "body": "A futures contract requires no investment. In a risk-neutral world its expected gain must therefore be zero, and the futures price has an expected growth rate of zero. An asset that provides a yield at rate \\(q\\) has a price that grows at \\(r-q\\) in a risk-neutral world; the futures price behaves as such an asset with \\(q=r\\). Setting \\(q=r\\) and \\(S_0=F_0\\) in Result 13.1 gives the following.\nResult 13.2 — Black's model for European futures options\n\\[ c=e^{-rT}\\left[F_0N(d_1)-KN(d_2)\\right],\\qquad p=e^{-rT}\\left[KN(-d_2)-F_0N(-d_1)\\right], \\] \\[ d_1=\\frac{\\ln(F_0/K)+\\sigma^{2}T/2}{\\sigma\\sqrt T},\\qquad d_2=d_1-\\sigma\\sqrt T, \\] where \\(F_0\\) is the current futures price and \\(\\sigma\\) the volatility of the futures price. Put–call parity takes the form \\(c+Ke^{-rT}=p+F_0e^{-rT}\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.4#20", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.4", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.4", "heading": "Valuation: Black's model", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 20, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.4 Valuation: Black's model", "body": "The model was published by Fischer Black in 1976. Its formulas are identical to those obtained for currency options in terms of the forward rate, and the agreement is general: whenever a European option expires at the same time as a futures or forward contract on the same asset, the option may be valued as an option on that contract, because the futures price and the spot price coincide at expiration. Practitioners value most European options in this way, since the forward price summarizes the interest rate, the income on the asset, and the storage cost in a single observable number."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.4#21", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.4", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.4", "heading": "Valuation: Black's model", "kind": "example", "ord": 21, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.4 Valuation: Black's model", "body": "Worked example 13.3 — A cap on the price of copper\nThe six-month copper futures price is $4.10 per pound, with a volatility of 22 percent. The risk-free rate is 5 percent. Northwind, which will buy 500,000 pounds in six months, considers buying European calls on the futures with a strike of $4.20 in place of the futures themselves. Value the call and the corresponding put, and state the maximum price that Northwind will pay for its copper.\n1\nPreliminaries. \\(\\ln(4.10/4.20)=-0.02410\\); \\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.22\\times0.7071=0.15556\\); \\(\\sigma^{2}T/2=0.0121\\); \\(e^{-rT}=0.975310\\).\n2\n\\(d_1\\) and \\(d_2\\). \\(d_1=(-0.02410+0.0121)/0.15556=-0.0771\\); \\(d_2=-0.2327\\). \\(N(d_1)=0.4693\\); \\(N(d_2)=0.4080\\).\n3\nCall. \\(c=0.975310\\times(4.10\\times0.4693-4.20\\times0.4080)=0.975310\\times0.2104=\\$0.2052\\) per pound.\n4\nPut, by parity. \\(p=c+(K-F_0)e^{-rT}=0.2052+0.10\\times0.975310=\\$0.3027\\).\n5"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.4#22", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.4", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.4", "heading": "Valuation: Black's model", "kind": "example", "ord": 22, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.4 Valuation: Black's model", "body": "The hedge. Calls on 500,000 pounds cost \\(500{,}000\\times0.2052=\\$102{,}600\\). Northwind will pay no more than $4.20 per pound, or $4.405 including the premium, and will pay the market price if copper falls. The futures hedge of Chapter 1 fixed the price at $4.10 at no cost; the call costs $0.2052 and preserves the benefit of a fall."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.4#23", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.4", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.4", "heading": "American futures options", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 23, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.4 American futures options", "body": "Traded futures options are American, and Result 13.2 gives only an approximation to their value. Unlike a call on a share that pays no dividend, an American call on a futures contract may be worth exercising early: when the call is deep in the money, exercise releases cash of \\(F-K\\) at once, on which interest can be earned, and nothing is given up in exchange, since the futures position costs nothing to hold. American futures options are therefore always worth more than the corresponding European options, and are valued on a binomial tree with \\(a=1\\), as in Table 10.1."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.5#24", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.5", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.5", "heading": "A single formula", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 24, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.5 A single formula", "body": "The four cases treated in Chapters 12 and 13 are special cases of Result 13.1, distinguished only by the value of \\(q\\).\nTable 13.1 European options: the parameters of Result 13.1 for four underlying assets\nUnderlying asset\\(S_0\\)\\(q\\)Growth rate of the price in a risk-neutral worldPut–call parity\nShare paying no dividendShare price0\\(r\\)\\(c+Ke^{-rT}=p+S_0\\)\nStock indexIndex levelDividend yield\\(r-q\\)\\(c+Ke^{-rT}=p+S_0e^{-qT}\\)\nCurrencyExchange rate\\(r_f\\)\\(r-r_f\\)\\(c+Ke^{-rT}=p+S_0e^{-r_fT}\\)\nFutures contractFutures price \\(F_0\\)\\(r\\)0\\(c+Ke^{-rT}=p+F_0e^{-rT}\\)\nIn every case the option is discounted at \\(r\\), and the only quantity that changes is the rate at which the underlying price is expected to grow in a risk-neutral world. That rate is the cost of carry of Section 4.7, net of any convenience yield: the same quantity that determines the forward price of the asset determines the adjustment to its options."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.5#25", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.5", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.5", "heading": "A single formula", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 25, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.5 A single formula", "body": "⚙ Interactive figure 13.2 — One formula, four underlying assets\nEuropean options on a share, an index, a currency, and a futures contract\nSelect an underlying asset to load the example of this chapter or of Chapter 12, then vary the inputs. Prices are expressed as percentages of the strike so that the four cases may be compared. For a futures contract the yield is held equal to the risk-free rate."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.5#26", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.5", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.5", "heading": "A single formula", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 26, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.5 A single formula", "body": "Concept check 13.3\nA European call and a European put on the same futures contract have the same strike and expiration, and the strike equals the current futures price. How do their prices compare?"}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.5#27", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.5", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.5", "heading": "A single formula", "kind": "notes", "ord": 27, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.5 A single formula", "body": "Historical note\nTrading in options on a stock index began in Chicago in March 1983 with a contract on what is now the S&P 100; options on the S&P 500 followed in July of that year. Portfolio insurance became a large business within a few years, though most of it was conducted not with traded puts but by replicating a put through sales of index futures as the market fell, in the manner of the dynamic hedge of Chapter 10. On 19 October 1987 the index fell by more than 20 percent in a single day. The sales required by these programs could not be executed at the prices assumed, the protection failed when it was needed, and the selling itself contributed to the fall. The episode remains the principal illustration of the difference between owning an option and attempting to manufacture one.\nMark Garman and Steven Kohlhagen published the currency form of the model in 1983, shortly after the Philadelphia Stock Exchange had listed the first options on currencies. Black's model of 1976 was devised for options on commodity futures, and became in the following decade the standard for interest rate options, a use that its author had not anticipated."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.summary#28", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.summary", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.summary", "heading": "Summary", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 28, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.summary Summary", "body": "A European option on an asset that provides a yield \\(q\\) is valued by replacing \\(S_0\\) with \\(S_0e^{-qT}\\) in the results for a share that pays no dividend; in the formulas, \\(r-q\\) replaces \\(r\\) in \\(d_1\\).\nA stock index is treated as an asset with a yield equal to its dividend yield. A portfolio with a beta of 1.0 is insured with \\(V/(100S_0)\\) put contracts.\nA currency is an asset with a yield equal to the foreign risk-free rate. A call on one currency is a put on the other.\nA futures option, when exercised, gives a futures position and cash. European futures options are valued by Black's model, which is Result 13.1 with \\(q=r\\); American futures options are worth more, and early exercise of both calls and puts may be optimal.\nAny European option that expires when a forward or futures contract on the same asset matures may be valued by Black's model, using the forward price."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.exercises#29", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.exercises", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 29, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.exercises Exercises", "body": "13.1 A stock index stands at 5,000, with a volatility of 20 percent and a dividend yield of 2 percent. The risk-free rate is 5 percent. Value a six-month European call with a strike of 5,000.\n\\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.1414\\). \\(d_1=[0+(0.05-0.02+0.02)\\times0.5]/0.1414=0.1768\\); \\(d_2=0.0354\\). \\(N(d_1)=0.5702\\); \\(N(d_2)=0.5141\\). \\(S_0e^{-qT}=5{,}000e^{-0.01}=4{,}950.25\\); \\(Ke^{-rT}=5{,}000e^{-0.025}=4{,}876.55\\). \\(c=4{,}950.25\\times0.5702-4{,}876.55\\times0.5141=315.4\\) index points.\n13.2 Use put–call parity to find the value of the put with the same terms as the call of Exercise 13.1.\n\\(p=c+Ke^{-rT}-S_0e^{-qT}=315.4+4{,}876.55-4{,}950.25=241.7\\) index points.\n13.3 The pound trades at $1.2500. The risk-free rates are 4 percent in dollars and 3 percent in sterling, and the volatility of the exchange rate is 10 percent. A United States company will receive £1,000,000 in three months. Find the cost of European puts on the pound with a strike of $1.2500 that protect the whole amount."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.exercises#30", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.exercises", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 30, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.exercises Exercises", "body": "\\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.05\\). \\(d_1=[0+(0.04-0.03+0.005)\\times0.25]/0.05=0.075\\); \\(d_2=0.025\\). \\(N(-d_1)=0.4701\\); \\(N(-d_2)=0.4900\\). \\(Ke^{-rT}=1.25e^{-0.01}=1.23756\\); \\(S_0e^{-r_fT}=1.25e^{-0.0075}=1.24066\\). \\(p=1.23756\\times0.4900-1.24066\\times0.4701=\\$0.0232\\) per pound; total \\(\\$23{,}200\\).\n13.4 A trader holds a put option on one crude oil futures contract (1,000 barrels) with a strike of $74. The trader exercises when the most recent settlement futures price is $69.50. What does the trader receive?\nA short position in one futures contract and cash of \\((74.00-69.50)\\times1{,}000=\\$4{,}500\\).\n13.5 A futures price is $75 and its volatility 30 percent. The risk-free rate is 4 percent. Value a three-month European call on the futures with a strike of $70, and find the put by parity.\n\\(\\sigma\\sqrt T=0.15\\); \\(\\sigma^{2}T/2=0.01125\\). \\(d_1=[\\ln(75/70)+0.01125]/0.15=(0.06899+0.01125)/0.15=0.5350\\); \\(d_2=0.3850\\). \\(N(d_1)=0.7037\\); \\(N(d_2)=0.6499\\). \\(c=e^{-0.01}\\times(75\\times0.7037-70\\times0.6499)=0.99005\\times7.284=\\$7.21\\). \\(p=c-(F_0-K)e^{-rT}=7.21-5\\times0.99005=\\$2.26\\)."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.exercises#31", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.exercises", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 31, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.exercises Exercises", "body": "13.6 A fund holds a portfolio worth $12,000,000 with a beta of 1.5. The index stands at 6,000; the risk-free rate is 1 percent per quarter and the dividend yield on the index 0.3 percent per quarter. The fund wishes to ensure that the portfolio is worth at least $10,800,000 in three months. Find the number of put contracts (multiplier $100) and their strike.\nContracts: \\(1.5\\times12{,}000{,}000/(100\\times6{,}000)=30\\). The floor corresponds to a portfolio return of −10 percent. From Section 5.5, the return on the portfolio is \\(1+1.5\\times(R_M-1)\\) percent, where \\(R_M\\) is the return on the index including dividends; setting this to −10 gives \\(R_M=-6.33\\) percent. Excluding the dividend of 0.3 percent, the index falls by 6.63 percent, to \\(6{,}000\\times0.9337=5{,}602\\). The fund buys 30 puts with a strike of about 5,600.\n13.7 Explain why it can be optimal to exercise an American call on a futures contract early, whereas it is never optimal to exercise early an American call on a share that pays no dividend."}, {"chunk_key": "fd-textbook:13.exercises#32", "source_key": "fd-textbook:13.exercises", "chapter": 13, "section": "13.exercises", "heading": "Exercises", "kind": "lesson", "ord": 32, "title": "Chapter 13, Options on Indices, Currencies, and Futures — 13.exercises Exercises", "body": "Exercising a call on a share requires payment of the strike, on which interest is then forgone; the lower bound \\(S_0-Ke^{-rT}\\) exceeds the exercise value \\(S_0-K\\). Exercising a call on a futures contract requires no payment: the holder receives \\(F-K\\) in cash and a futures position that costs nothing. The lower bound for the European futures call is \\((F_0-K)e^{-rT}\\), which is less than the exercise value \\(F_0-K\\). When the call is deep in the money and has little time value, receiving the cash at once is worth more than waiting.\n13.8 Show that the Garman–Kohlhagen formula for a currency call and Black's formula for a call on the corresponding forward contract give the same price.\nWith \\(F_0=S_0e^{(r-r_f)T}\\): \\(S_0e^{-r_fT}=F_0e^{-rT}\\), so the first term \\(S_0e^{-r_fT}N(d_1)\\) equals \\(e^{-rT}F_0N(d_1)\\). In \\(d_1\\), \\(\\ln(S_0/K)+(r-r_f)T=\\ln(F_0/K)\\), so \\(d_1=[\\ln(F_0/K)+\\sigma^{2}T/2]/(\\sigma\\sqrt T)\\), which is the \\(d_1\\) of Black's model. The second term, \\(Ke^{-rT}N(d_2)\\), is common to both. Hence \\(c=e^{-rT}[F_0N(d_1)-KN(d_2)]\\) in each case."}]$aim_json$::jsonb, 'Financial Derivatives (MacroNations textbook)');
